# ── Coverage derivation, landed 2026-07-31 ───────────────────────────────────
# The first design proved a coverage claim by PLANTING a defect and requiring the
# command to catch it — carrying "released by making it fail" over from maintainers,
# where it is right. The carry-over was wrong: a planted defect proves ONE POINT
# inside a claim about a WHOLE SCOPE, so the evidence never matched the shape of the
# claim. It was expensive, destructive, and weak at once.
#
# The fix is not a better audit of the cell. It is deriving the cell, so there is no
# authored claim left to be wrong — over-claim becomes unreachable rather than
# detected. Commands enumerate themselves for free and non-destructively, and that
# also dissolves the source-drift blind spot the stamp was invented to tolerate.
check(
    "org-chart-format: Covers is derived from the command, never authored",
    "DERIVED, never authored" in _ocf and "Nothing authors the cell" in _ocf,
    "an authored coverage claim can over-claim, and an over-claim is the one error "
    "that produces a false PASS at dispatch — deriving it removes the cell that "
    "could have been wrong",
)
check(
    "org: only a derived scope may satisfy total coverage",
    "only if its `Scope` cell says `derived`" in _org_block
    and "`derived <date>`" in _ocf and "step only" in _ocf,
    "a `declared` row rests on prose somebody wrote; letting it answer an ask alone "
    "reinstates exactly the authored claim derivation exists to remove",
)
check(
